{"i":0,"status":"fulfilled","value":{"chunk_id":"95fcf9","wall_time_seconds":0.00000821,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":2585,"output":"Dual-energy / modified-entropy contingency: bounded independent literature review

Conclusion. Scientifically feasible as a separate, explicitly nonconservative numerical contingency for unreliable thermal-energy subtraction. It is not a demonstrated cure for this solver's cold cells, divergence-force error, or heating model. Finish the active L=1 control first; no implementation is recommended solely because L=.18 remains positive with a .768K minimum. The reported .768K/n=.573 and nearest-comet residual-force ratio~4.5% are parent-provided context, not measurements independently rerun here. No solver code, GPU jobs, model runs, or floor prescriptions were made.

Primary implementation evidence. Li/Li/Cen's CosmoMHD §3.3 evolves either modified entropy S=p/rho^(gamma-1) or internal energy alongside conservative MHD energy, switches when internal/total energy is small, and rebuilds total energy from the auxiliary thermal estimate. Its tested MHD eta is .008, not .001; the paper discusses shock-detection choices and the need to retain total-energy shock heating. [https://arxiv.org/pdf/astro-ph/0611863]
Bryan et al.'s Enzo §4.1.1 equations44-45 use eta1=.001 for pressure selection and a separate neighborhood-based eta2=.1 for resynchronization of the auxiliary internal energy. These are empirical choices for that algorithm, not validation of a single-threshold entropy switch in our FP32 GLM/Janhunen method. [https://arxiv.org/pdf/1307.2265]
The PLUTO developer manual §2.2.4 explicitly evolves rho*(p/rho^gamma) as a conservative passive scalar, derives pressure from energy or entropy selectively, and uses converging flow plus normalized pressure gradients to identify shocks. It warns that selective switching generally conserves neither total energy nor the entropy tracer exactly. [https://plutocode.ph.unito.it/userguide.pdf]
BATS-R-US's author-hosted space-weather methods paper §2.3.2 documents switching between energy and pressure formulations in a production space-weather MHD framework; this is precedent for a hybrid, not evidence that all such variants or thresholds are equivalent. [https://websites.umich.edu/~gtoth/Papers/Toth2012review.pdf]

Consistency with this literal PDE. Define A=p/rho^gamma and S=rho*A. With spatially uniform cleaning speed, E=e+K+M+Q, e=p/(gamma-1), K=rho*v^2/2, M=B^2/2 and Q=q^2/2, our smooth induction-only Janhunen correction cancels the conservative-divB pressure forcing. Thus DA/Dt=0 and S_t+div(S*v)=0 in smooth ideal flow. Consistent gravity changes kinetic/total energy together; the matched q and q-energy damping preserves gas pressure. Neither supplies a gas-entropy source. This is a continuum statement, not an exact discrete product rule or entropy-stability proof for the centered source. The prior Janhunen review gives its assumptions. Wu/Shu AppendixA/RemarkA.1 supports the smooth pressure identity. [https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.11467]
Using F_S=F_mass*A_upwind on the SAME shared face, stage, fallback and geometry is a consistent finite-volume passive-scalar realization (an inference from the conservation law and standard scalar transport, not a claim to reproduce all cited solvers). Initialize and fill S from the actual stage's density/pressure, including ambient closure and any CME boundary changes. No spherical geometric source is needed beyond the existing face areas and volumes. Positive S with positive rho gives p_S=S*rho^(gamma-1); scalar reconstruction/staging still needs its own positivity check.

Switch and energy accounting. Recover e_E=E-K-M-Q and p_E=(gamma-1)*e_E. A conservative/entropy pressure switch must define its reliability ratio precisely, including magnetic AND cleaning energy in the cancellation budget. A ratio e_E/E is conventional but not sufficient evidence of reliability, particularly for accumulated truncation/divergence errors. Compare the cold-state subtraction in adequate precision and compare with the auxiliary adiabat; merely re-evaluating already-rounded FP32 E in FP64 cannot recover information already lost.
Where conservative thermal energy is reliable, reset S from p_E so shock/dissipation heating is retained. Where it is unreliable and the flow is smooth, use p_S; to keep the stored state thermodynamically consistent, set E_new=K+M+Q+p_S/(gamma-1). Record DeltaE=E_new-E_conservative as an explicit correction, not as gravity, GLM damping, or physical heating. If pressure alone changes while E remains untouched, the code has inconsistent thermodynamic closures and still needs an explicit accounting/qualification policy. Energy repair affects the primitive/CFL cache and all stages/rollback, so it is not an output-only option.
At shocks, smooth entropy advection lacks irreversible entropy production. A blanket entropy choice can erase shock heating, and resynchronization from unreliable energy can poison the tracer. Published shock criteria and synchronization thresholds differ. MHD fast/slow shocks, compressions and tangential discontinuities must be distinguished; compression alone or a gas-pressure jump alone is not a universal shock classifier. If both the energy pressure and the shock-safe auxiliary estimate are invalid, retain rejection/repair or an explicit failure rather than asserting that the entropy switch guarantees a valid solution.

Minimum meaningful qualification if the contingency becomes necessary:
1. Default-disabled parent identity and adequate-precision implementation agreement. Uniform/contact/entropy advection and a cold smooth rarefaction/radial adiabat at gamma1.5 must preserve the expected entropy. Consistent gravity and nonzero-q cleaning/damping must not invent gas heat. Exercise rejected-stage rollback of S, synchronization, E-correction ledger and cached CFL.
2. Strong hydro and MHD fast/slow shock tests with cold boosted upstream states must recover conservative reference jumps and entropy increases. Include local-HLLE/global fallback paths; a smooth-only entropy test does not establish shock behavior.
3. Keep separate accepted-state ledgers for S advection/resynchronization, signed and absolute volume-integrated DeltaE, maxima/cell counts and where/when corrections occur. Normalize corrections to thermal as well as total energy and retain the existing boundary/gravity/q-damping/B-source ledgers. A tiny global DeltaE/E can hide order-unity thermal changes.
4. In a bounded representative wind/CIR case covering the previously cold cells and the comet interval, vary eta (and any separate synchronization/shock threshold), timestep and resolution, and compare n/v/incident flux/temperature/force-divB with the best conservative adequate-precision control. At least bracket .001; .008 is a published MHD comparison point, not a recommended value for us. Corrections and target quantities must become acceptably insensitive; survival/positive pressure alone is insufficient.

Limits. This auxiliary equation does not provide real coronal/heliospheric heating absent from the model and does not remove Janhunen's residual-divB momentum force. It can mask an inconsistent magnetic-energy/source discretization if imposed too broadly. A successful hybrid would be a new numerical formulation with measured energy corrections, not an exactly conservative continuation of previous cases or proof of physical/numerical convergence. The active damping-length control may obviate it; retain the contingency without starting a new implementation project now.
